ghts  civil rights human rights lgbtq rights  or  kind of protecting the environment was  really  kind of going to be at least somewhat  determined by who would be on the court  and i you've said this before justice  scalia's passing and after justice  scalia's passing and  um after justice scalia's passing you  would always say  you know of course his loss was a  devastating uh  personal tragedy for his family and his  loved ones  we still have to have to be focused on  on the supreme court in this election  while  acknowledging and respecting that and i  was accused by the right of  you know dancing on justice scalia's  grave and being disrespectful  and so i have a little bit of whiplash  admittedly over the last  48 hours because kind of the reactions  certainly don't  appear to me to be consistent and i do  believe  that senator mcconnell certainly created  a precedent  when he refused a hearing for judge mark  garland  and and think although i don't expect  him to but certainly think that he  should abide by that yeah there seems to  be a little bit of amnesia in washington  these days we'll wait to see what's  going to happen there but  listen she would have been perfect well  listen in your first book she persisted  you don't have a better role model for  that book than than ruth bader ginsburg  and now you've turned your attention to  sports  number one there were so many things i  didn't know you know i knew some of the  athletes but to hear their backstories  like  wilma rudolph was born premature  prematurely she wore a leg brace yet she  became a champion runner  christy yamaguchi was born with a club  foot she became a champion skater and  then of course venus and serena williams  confronted racism and sexism they  persisted  so your first line in the book says this  it's not easy to be a girl  athlete how come  well i think uh for all of the kind of  women's stories you just  shared and countless others i mean you  know my grandmother gail who you know  was a  huge huge influence on my mom and on me  um spoke a lot about how inspired she'd  been by  two women that i am kind of so honored  to include in she persisted in sports  um dave zaharias and and wilma rudolph  and  she spoke about just how um how much it  inspired her  as a you know kind of uh middle-class  chicago housewife seeing wilma rudolph  so unapologetically  be this fierce athlete and to kind of  represent our country in the olympics  and that she had been a teenage mother  that she had overcome  so many uh challenges in her young life  including multiple kind of illnesses and  sicknesses  and and polio that kind of left her her  legs weekend  um and so i just i grew up surrounded by  these stories of women breaking barriers  including  uh in sports and it's never been easy it  still isn't easy  um and yet we see our women athletes  leading like  uh naomi osaka did so spectacularly at  the u.s open  you know just a few days ago yeah yeah  hey chelsea  tony decouple here uh books matter this  book will matter to  to a lot of readers uh but sports matter  too and that's one of the messages of  this book you played soccer growing up i  think we have a picture of it  if we do let's put it up on camera there  you are  i'm so glad that i could just hear you  and not see you so i'm not like seeing  like the millions of people watching  are seeing my like awkward awkward years  but at least like  in my living room i'm not seeing much  you're welcome chelsea  yeah so the question is i mean thank you  gail thank you thanks for looking out  what did sports mean to you growing up  oh my gosh i loved sports  and i played sports i took ballet and i  just i  found um the camaraderie of being in a  team  kind of learning how to work as a team  kind of learning how to be my best  both to be my best and to be the best  for my team just such like an invaluable  set of lessons um for when i was a kid  and still today and you know when i  think about  you know what i 